  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given two integers NNN and MMM. You have to construct a matrix with NNN rows and MMM columns. Consider a multiset SSS which contains N+MN + MN+M integers: for each row and each column of this matrix, the MEX of the elements of this row/column belongs to SSS. Then, in the matrix you construct, the MEX of SSS must be maximum possible.\n\nNote: The MEX of a multiset of integers is the smallest non-negative integer that is not present in this multiset. For example, MEX({4,9,0,1,1,5})=2\\mathrm{MEX}(\\{4,9,0,1,1,5\\}) = 2MEX({4,9,0,1,1,5})=2 and MEX({1,2,3})=0\\mathrm{MEX}(\\{1,2,3\\}) = 0MEX({1,2,3})=0.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤4001 \\le T \\le 4001≤T≤400",
      "1≤N,M≤1001 \\le N, M \\le 1001≤N,M≤100",
      "the sum of N⋅MN \\cdot MN⋅M over all test cases does not exceed 10510^5105",
      "Example Input`2",
      "3 3",
      "4 2",
      "`Example Output`9 1 0",
      "1 0 2",
      "7 5 8",
      "2 2",
      "3 0",
      "1 1",
      "0 1",
      "`Explanation**Example case 1:** The MEXs of the rows {9,1,0}\\{9, 1, 0\\}{9,1,0}, {1,0,2}\\{1, 0, 2\\}{1,0,2} and {7,5,8}\\{7, 5, 8\\}{7,5,8} are 222, 333 and 000 respectively. The MEXs of the columns {9,1,7}\\{9, 1, 7\\}{9,1,7}, {1,0,5}\\{1, 0, 5\\}{1,0,5} and {0,2,8}\\{0, 2, 8\\}{0,2,8} are 000, 222 and 111 respectively. Therefore, the multiset which contains the MEX of each row and each column is S={0,0,1,2,2,3}S = \\{0, 0, 1, 2, 2, 3\\}S={0,0,1,2,2,3} and MEX(S)=4\\mathrm{MEX}(S) = 4MEX(S)=4. We can show that this is the largest MEX that can be obtained for a 3×33 \\times 33×3 matrix."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print NNN lines. For each iii (1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N), the iii-th of these lines should contain MMM space-separated integers ― the elements in the iii-th row of your matrix. Each element of the matrix must be non-negative and must not exceed 10910^9109.\n\nIf there are multiple solutions, you may find any one of them.",
    "notes": [],
